window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2016-06-3", "source": "2016年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on e-learning. Try to imagine what will happen when more and more people study online instead of attending school. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "E-learning is quickly becoming a part of everyday life, and it is likely that in the near future many people will\nstudy entirely online rather than walk into a classroom. When that day comes, our idea of \"going to school\"\nwill change dramatically.\nOn the bright side, learning will grow far more flexible and inclusive. We can foresee students attending live\nlectures from home, receiving AI-powered personalized tutoring, and running experiments in virtual\nlaboratories, so quality education will reach even remote villages. Yet the picture will not be entirely rosy.\nWithout face-to-face interaction, learners may feel isolated, self-discipline will matter more than ever, and the\nwarm social life of campus, clubs, friendships and shared canteens, may gradually fade away.\nIn the long run, I believe online study will not fully replace schools but will merge with them. The wisest path\nis to enjoy the convenience technology offers while deliberately preserving the human connection that no\nscreen can truly reproduce."}, "translation": {"prompt": "深圳是中国广东省一座新开发的城市。在改革开放之前,深圳不过是一个渔村,仅有三万多人。20世纪80年代,中国政府创建了深圳经济特区,作为实施社会主义市场经济的试验田。如今,深圳的人口已经超过1,000万,整个城市发生了巨大的变化。 到2014年,深圳的人均 (per-capita) GDP已达25,000美元,相当于世界上一些发达国家的水平。就综合经济实力而言,深圳居于中国顶尖城市之列。由于其独特的地位,深圳也是国内外企业家创业的理想之地。", "sample": "Shenzhen is a newly developed city in Guangdong Province, China. Before the reform and opening- up, it was no more than a fishing village with a population of just over 30,000. In the 1980s, the Chinese government established the Shenzhen Special Economic Zone as a testing ground for implementing the socialist market economy. Today, Shenzhen's population has exceeded 10 million, and the whole city has undergone tremendous changes. By 2014, its per-capita GDP had reached 25,000 US dollars, equivalent to the level of some developed countries in the world. In terms of its overall economic strength, Shenzhen ranks among the top cities in China. Thanks to its unique status, it is also an ideal place for entrepreneurs at home and abroad to start their businesses."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "The robotics revolution is set to bring humans face to face with an old fear—man-made creations as smart and capable as we are but without a moral compass. As robots take on ever more complex roles, the question naturally  __26__  : Who will be responsible when they do something wrong? Manufacturers? Users? Software writers? The answer depends on the robot.\nRobots already save us time, money and energy. In the future, they will improve our health care, social welfare and standard of living. The  __27__  of computational power and engineering advances will  __28__  enable lower-cost in-home care for the disabled,  __29__  use of driverless cars that may reduce drunk- and distracted-driving accidents and countless home and service-industry uses for robots, from street cleaning to food preparation.\nBut there are  __30__  to be problems. Robot cars will crash. A drone(遥控飞行器) operator will  __31__  someone's privacy. A robotic lawn mower(割草机) will run over a neighbor's cat. Juries sympathetic to the  __32__  of machines will punish entrepreneurs with company-crushing  __33__  and damages. What should governments do to protect people while  __34__  space for innovation?\nBig, complicated systems on which much public safety depends, like driverless cars, should be built,  __35__  and sold by manufacturers who take responsibility for ensuring safety and are liable for accidents. Governments should set safety requirements and then let insurers price the risk of the robots based on the manufacturer's driving record, not the passenger's.", "matchIntro": "Reform and Medical Costs", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Americans are deeply concerned about the relentless rise in health care costs and health insurance premiums. They need to know if reform will help solve the problem. The answer is that no one has an easy fix for rising medical costs. The fundamental fix—reshaping how care is delivered and how doctors are paid in a wasteful, abnormal system—is likely to be achieved only through trial and error and incremental(渐进的) gains."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The good news is that a bill just approved by the House and a bill approved by the Senate Finance Committee would implement or test many reforms that should help slow the rise in medical costs over the long term. As a report in The New England Journal of Medicine concluded, \"Pretty much every proposed innovation found in the health policy literature these days is contained in these measures.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Medical spending, which typically rises faster than wages and the overall economy, is propelled by two things: the high prices charged for medical services in this country and the volume of unnecessary care delivered by doctors and hospitals, which often perform a lot more tests and treatments than a patient really needs."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Here are some of the important proposals in the House and Senate bills to try to address those problems, and why it is hard to know how well they will work."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Both bills would reduce the rate of growth in annual Medicare payments to hospitals, nursing homes and other providers by amounts comparable to the productivity savings routinely made in other industries with the help of new technologies and new ways to organize work. This proposal could save Medicare more than $100 billion over the next decade. If private plans demanded similar productivity savings from providers, and refused to let providers shift additional costs to them, the savings could be much larger. Critics say Congress will give in to lobbyists and let inefficient providers off the hook(放过). That is far less likely to happen if Congress also adopts strong \"pay-go\" rules requiring that any increase in payments to providers be offset by new taxes or budget cuts."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The Senate Finance bill would impose an excise tax(消费税) on health insurance plans that cost more than $8,000 for an individual or $21,000 for a family. It would most likely cause insurers to redesign plans to fall beneath the threshold. Enrollees would have to pay more money for many services out of their own pockets, and that would encourage them to think twice about whether an expensive or redundant test was worth it. Economists project that most employers would shift money from expensive health benefits into wages. The House bill has no similar tax. The final legislation should."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Any doctor who has wrestled with multiple forms from different insurers, or patients who have tried to understand their own parade of statements, know that simplification ought to save money. When the health insurance industry was still cooperating in reform efforts, its trade group offered to provide standardized forms for automated processing. It estimated that step would save hundreds of billions of dollars over the next decade. The bills would lock that pledge into law."}, {"label": "H", "text": "The stimulus package provided money to convert the inefficient, paper-driven medical system to electronic records that can be easily viewed and transmitted. This requires open investments to help doctors convert. In time it should help restrain costs by eliminating redundant tests, preventing drug interactions, and helping doctors find the best treatments."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Virtually all experts agree that the fee-for-service system—doctors are rewarded for the quantity of care rather than its quality or effectiveness—is a primary reason that the cost of care is so high. Most agree that the solution is to push doctors to accept fixed payments to care for a particular illness or for a patient's needs over a year. No one knows how to make that happen quickly. The bills in both houses would start pilot projects within Medicare. They include such measures as accountable care organizations to take charge of a patient's needs with an eye on both cost and quality, and chronic disease management to make sure the seriously ill, who are responsible for the bulk of all health care costs, are treated properly. For the most part, these experiments rely on incentive payments to get doctors to try them."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Testing innovations do no good unless the good experiments are identified and expanded and the bad ones are dropped. The Senate bill would create an independent commission to monitor the pilot programs and recommend changes in Medicare's payment policies to urge providers to adopt reforms that work. The changes would have to be approved or rejected as a whole by Congress, making it hard for narrow-interest lobbies to bend lawmakers to their will."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The bills in both chambers would create health insurance exchanges on which small businesses and individuals could choose from an array of private plans and possibly a public option. All the plans would have to provide standard benefit packages that would be easy to compare. To get access to millions of new customers, insurers would have a strong incentive to lower their prices, perhaps by accepting slimmer profit margins or demanding better deals from providers."}, {"label": "L", "text": "The final legislation might throw a public plan into the competition, but thanks to the fierce opposition of the insurance industry and Republican critics, it might not save much money. The one in the House bill would have to negotiate rates with providers, rather than using Medicare rates, as many reformers wanted."}, {"label": "M", "text": "The president's stimulus package is pumping money into research to compare how well various treatments work. Is surgery, radiation or careful monitoring best for prostate(前列腺) cancer? Is the latest and most expensive cholesterol-lowering drug any better than its common competitors? The pending bills would spend additional money to accelerate this effort."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Critics have charged that this sensible idea would lead to rationing of care. (That would be true only if you believed that patients should have an unrestrained right to treatments proven to be inferior.) As a result, the bills do not require, as they should, that the results of these studies be used to set payment rates in Medicare."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Congress needs to find the courage to allow Medicare to pay preferentially for treatments proven to be superior. Sometimes the best treatment might be the most expensive. But overall, we suspect that spending would come down through elimination of a lot of unnecessary or even dangerous tests and treatments."}, {"label": "P", "text": "The House bill would authorize the secretary of health and human services to negotiate drug prices in Medicare and Medicaid. Some authoritative analysts doubt that the secretary would get better deals than private insurers already get. We believe negotiation could work. It does in other countries."}, {"label": "Q", "text": "Missing from these bills is any serious attempt to rein in malpractice costs. Malpractice awards do drive up insurance premiums for doctors in high⁃risk specialties, and there is some evidence that doctors engage in \"defensive medicine\" by performing tests and treatments primarily to prove they are not negligent should they get sued."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Facing water shortages and escalating fertilizer costs, farmers in developing countries are using raw sewage(下水道污水) to irrigate and fertilize nearly 49 million acres of cropland, according to a new report—and it may not be a bad thing.\nWhile the practice carries serious health risks for many, those dangers are outweighed by the social and economic gains for poor urban farmers and consumers who need affordable food.\n\"There is a large potential for wastewater agriculture to both help and hurt great numbers of urban consumers,\" said Liqa Raschid-Sally, who led the study.\nThe report focused on poor urban areas, where farms in or near cities supply relatively inexpensive food. Most of these operations draw irrigation water from local rivers or lakes. Unlike developed cities, however, these areas lack advanced water-treatment facilities, and rivers effectively become sewers(下水道).\nWhen this water is used for agricultural irrigation, farmers risk absorbing disease-causing bacteria, as do consumers who eat the produce raw and unwashed. Nearly 2.2 million people die each year because of diarrhea-related(与腹泻相关的) diseases, according to WHO statistics. More than 80% of those cases can be attributed to contact with contaminated water and a lack of proper sanitation. But Pay Drechsel, an environmental scientist, argues that the social and economic benefits of using untreated human waste to grow food outweigh the health risks.\nThose dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free water and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to escape poverty.\nAgriculture is a water-intensive business, accounting for nearly 70% of global fresh water consumption.\nIn poor, dry regions, untreated wastewater is the only viable irrigation source to keep farmers in business. In some cases, water is so scarce that farmers break open sewage pipes transporting waste to local rivers.\nIrrigation is the primary agricultural use of human waste in the developing world. But frequently untreated human waste harvested from lavatories is delivered to farms and spread as fertilizer.\nIn most cases, the human waste is used on grain crops, which are eventually cooked, minimizing the risk of transmitting water-borne diseases. With fertilizer prices jumping nearly 50% per metric ton over the last year in some places, human waste is an attractive, and often necessary, alternative.\nIn cases where sewage mud is used, expensive chemical fertilizer use can be avoided. The mud contains the same critical nutrients.\n\"Overly strict standards often fail,\" James Bartram, a WHO water-health expert, said. \"We need to accept that fact across much of the planet, so waste with little or no treatment will be used in agriculture for good reason.\"", "47": "Facing water shortages and escalating fertilizer costs, farmers in developing countries are using raw sewage(下水道污水) to irrigate and fertilize nearly 49 million acres of cropland, according to a new report—and it may not be a bad thing.\nWhile the practice carries serious health risks for many, those dangers are outweighed by the social and economic gains for poor urban farmers and consumers who need affordable food.\n\"There is a large potential for wastewater agriculture to both help and hurt great numbers of urban consumers,\" said Liqa Raschid-Sally, who led the study.\nThe report focused on poor urban areas, where farms in or near cities supply relatively inexpensive food. Most of these operations draw irrigation water from local rivers or lakes. Unlike developed cities, however, these areas lack advanced water-treatment facilities, and rivers effectively become sewers(下水道).\nWhen this water is used for agricultural irrigation, farmers risk absorbing disease-causing bacteria, as do consumers who eat the produce raw and unwashed. Nearly 2.2 million people die each year because of diarrhea-related(与腹泻相关的) diseases, according to WHO statistics. More than 80% of those cases can be attributed to contact with contaminated water and a lack of proper sanitation. But Pay Drechsel, an environmental scientist, argues that the social and economic benefits of using untreated human waste to grow food outweigh the health risks.\nThose dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free water and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to escape poverty.\nAgriculture is a water-intensive business, accounting for nearly 70% of global fresh water consumption.\nIn poor, dry regions, untreated wastewater is the only viable irrigation source to keep farmers in business. In some cases, water is so scarce that farmers break open sewage pipes transporting waste to local rivers.\nIrrigation is the primary agricultural use of human waste in the developing world. But frequently untreated human waste harvested from lavatories is delivered to farms and spread as fertilizer.\nIn most cases, the human waste is used on grain crops, which are eventually cooked, minimizing the risk of transmitting water-borne diseases. With fertilizer prices jumping nearly 50% per metric ton over the last year in some places, human waste is an attractive, and often necessary, alternative.\nIn cases where sewage mud is used, expensive chemical fertilizer use can be avoided. The mud contains the same critical nutrients.\n\"Overly strict standards often fail,\" James Bartram, a WHO water-health expert, said. \"We need to accept that fact across much of the planet, so waste with little or no treatment will be used in agriculture for good reason.\"", "48": "Facing water shortages and escalating fertilizer costs, farmers in developing countries are using raw sewage(下水道污水) to irrigate and fertilize nearly 49 million acres of cropland, according to a new report—and it may not be a bad thing.\nWhile the practice carries serious health risks for many, those dangers are outweighed by the social and economic gains for poor urban farmers and consumers who need affordable food.\n\"There is a large potential for wastewater agriculture to both help and hurt great numbers of urban consumers,\" said Liqa Raschid-Sally, who led the study.\nThe report focused on poor urban areas, where farms in or near cities supply relatively inexpensive food. Most of these operations draw irrigation water from local rivers or lakes. Unlike developed cities, however, these areas lack advanced water-treatment facilities, and rivers effectively become sewers(下水道).\nWhen this water is used for agricultural irrigation, farmers risk absorbing disease-causing bacteria, as do consumers who eat the produce raw and unwashed. Nearly 2.2 million people die each year because of diarrhea-related(与腹泻相关的) diseases, according to WHO statistics. More than 80% of those cases can be attributed to contact with contaminated water and a lack of proper sanitation. But Pay Drechsel, an environmental scientist, argues that the social and economic benefits of using untreated human waste to grow food outweigh the health risks.\nThose dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free water and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to escape poverty.\nAgriculture is a water-intensive business, accounting for nearly 70% of global fresh water consumption.\nIn poor, dry regions, untreated wastewater is the only viable irrigation source to keep farmers in business. In some cases, water is so scarce that farmers break open sewage pipes transporting waste to local rivers.\nIrrigation is the primary agricultural use of human waste in the developing world. But frequently untreated human waste harvested from lavatories is delivered to farms and spread as fertilizer.\nIn most cases, the human waste is used on grain crops, which are eventually cooked, minimizing the risk of transmitting water-borne diseases. With fertilizer prices jumping nearly 50% per metric ton over the last year in some places, human waste is an attractive, and often necessary, alternative.\nIn cases where sewage mud is used, expensive chemical fertilizer use can be avoided. The mud contains the same critical nutrients.\n\"Overly strict standards often fail,\" James Bartram, a WHO water-health expert, said. \"We need to accept that fact across much of the planet, so waste with little or no treatment will be used in agriculture for good reason.\"", "49": "Facing water shortages and escalating fertilizer costs, farmers in developing countries are using raw sewage(下水道污水) to irrigate and fertilize nearly 49 million acres of cropland, according to a new report—and it may not be a bad thing.\nWhile the practice carries serious health risks for many, those dangers are outweighed by the social and economic gains for poor urban farmers and consumers who need affordable food.\n\"There is a large potential for wastewater agriculture to both help and hurt great numbers of urban consumers,\" said Liqa Raschid-Sally, who led the study.\nThe report focused on poor urban areas, where farms in or near cities supply relatively inexpensive food. Most of these operations draw irrigation water from local rivers or lakes. Unlike developed cities, however, these areas lack advanced water-treatment facilities, and rivers effectively become sewers(下水道).\nWhen this water is used for agricultural irrigation, farmers risk absorbing disease-causing bacteria, as do consumers who eat the produce raw and unwashed. Nearly 2.2 million people die each year because of diarrhea-related(与腹泻相关的) diseases, according to WHO statistics. More than 80% of those cases can be attributed to contact with contaminated water and a lack of proper sanitation. But Pay Drechsel, an environmental scientist, argues that the social and economic benefits of using untreated human waste to grow food outweigh the health risks.\nThose dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free water and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to escape poverty.\nAgriculture is a water-intensive business, accounting for nearly 70% of global fresh water consumption.\nIn poor, dry regions, untreated wastewater is the only viable irrigation source to keep farmers in business. In some cases, water is so scarce that farmers break open sewage pipes transporting waste to local rivers.\nIrrigation is the primary agricultural use of human waste in the developing world. But frequently untreated human waste harvested from lavatories is delivered to farms and spread as fertilizer.\nIn most cases, the human waste is used on grain crops, which are eventually cooked, minimizing the risk of transmitting water-borne diseases. With fertilizer prices jumping nearly 50% per metric ton over the last year in some places, human waste is an attractive, and often necessary, alternative.\nIn cases where sewage mud is used, expensive chemical fertilizer use can be avoided. The mud contains the same critical nutrients.\n\"Overly strict standards often fail,\" James Bartram, a WHO water-health expert, said. \"We need to accept that fact across much of the planet, so waste with little or no treatment will be used in agriculture for good reason.\"", "50": "Facing water shortages and escalating fertilizer costs, farmers in developing countries are using raw sewage(下水道污水) to irrigate and fertilize nearly 49 million acres of cropland, according to a new report—and it may not be a bad thing.\nWhile the practice carries serious health risks for many, those dangers are outweighed by the social and economic gains for poor urban farmers and consumers who need affordable food.\n\"There is a large potential for wastewater agriculture to both help and hurt great numbers of urban consumers,\" said Liqa Raschid-Sally, who led the study.\nThe report focused on poor urban areas, where farms in or near cities supply relatively inexpensive food. Most of these operations draw irrigation water from local rivers or lakes. Unlike developed cities, however, these areas lack advanced water-treatment facilities, and rivers effectively become sewers(下水道).\nWhen this water is used for agricultural irrigation, farmers risk absorbing disease-causing bacteria, as do consumers who eat the produce raw and unwashed. Nearly 2.2 million people die each year because of diarrhea-related(与腹泻相关的) diseases, according to WHO statistics. More than 80% of those cases can be attributed to contact with contaminated water and a lack of proper sanitation. But Pay Drechsel, an environmental scientist, argues that the social and economic benefits of using untreated human waste to grow food outweigh the health risks.\nThose dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free water and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to escape poverty.\nAgriculture is a water-intensive business, accounting for nearly 70% of global fresh water consumption.\nIn poor, dry regions, untreated wastewater is the only viable irrigation source to keep farmers in business. In some cases, water is so scarce that farmers break open sewage pipes transporting waste to local rivers.\nIrrigation is the primary agricultural use of human waste in the developing world. But frequently untreated human waste harvested from lavatories is delivered to farms and spread as fertilizer.\nIn most cases, the human waste is used on grain crops, which are eventually cooked, minimizing the risk of transmitting water-borne diseases. With fertilizer prices jumping nearly 50% per metric ton over the last year in some places, human waste is an attractive, and often necessary, alternative.\nIn cases where sewage mud is used, expensive chemical fertilizer use can be avoided. The mud contains the same critical nutrients.\n\"Overly strict standards often fail,\" James Bartram, a WHO water-health expert, said. \"We need to accept that fact across much of the planet, so waste with little or no treatment will be used in agriculture for good reason.\"", "51": "These days, nobody needs to cook. Families graze on high-cholesterol take-aways and microwaved ready-meals. Cooking is an occasional hobby and a vehicle for celebrity chefs. Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern house: what the great hall was to the medieval castle, the kitchen is to the 21st-century home.\nThe money spent on kitchens has risen with their status. In America the kitchen market is now worth $170 billion, five times the country's film industry. In the year to August 2007, IKEA, a Swedish furniture chain, sold over one million kitchens worldwide. The average budget for a \"major\" kitchen overhaul in 2006, calculates Remodeling magazine, was a staggering $54,000; even a \"minor\" improvement cost on average $18,000.\nExclusivity, more familiar in the world of high fashion, has reached the kitchen: Robinson & Cornish, a British manufacturer of custom-made kitchens, offers a Georgian-style one which would cost 145,000-155,000—excluding building, plumbing and electrical work. Its big selling point is that nobody else will have it: \"You won't see this kitchen anywhere else in the world.\"\nThe elevation of the room that once belonged only to the servants to that of design showcase for the modern family tells the story of a century of social change. Right into the early 20th century, kitchens were smoky, noisy places, generally located underground, or to the back of the house, and as far from living space as possible. That was as it should be: kitchens were for servants, and the aspiring middle classes wanted nothing to do with them.\nBut as the working classes prospered and the servant shortage set in, housekeeping became a matter of interest to the educated classes. One of the pioneers of a radical new way of thinking about the kitchen was Catharine Esther Beecher, sister of Harriet Beecher Stowe. In American Woman's Home, published in 1869, the Beecher sisters recommended a scientific approach to household management, designed to enhance the efficiency of a woman's work and promote order.\nMany contemporary ideas about kitchen design can be traced back to another American, Christine Frederick, who set about enhancing the efficiency of the housewife. Her 1919 work, Household Engineering: Scientific Management in the Home, was based on detailed observation of a housewife's daily routine. She borrowed the principle of efficiency on the factory floor and applied it to domestic tasks on the kitchen floor.\nFrederick's central idea, that \"stove, sink and kitchen table must be placed in such a relation that useless steps are avoided entirely\", inspired the first fully fitted kitchen, designed in the 1920s by Margarete Schütte-Lihotsky. It was a modernist triumph, and many elements remain central features of today's kitchen.", "52": "These days, nobody needs to cook. Families graze on high-cholesterol take-aways and microwaved ready-meals. Cooking is an occasional hobby and a vehicle for celebrity chefs. Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern house: what the great hall was to the medieval castle, the kitchen is to the 21st-century home.\nThe money spent on kitchens has risen with their status. In America the kitchen market is now worth $170 billion, five times the country's film industry. In the year to August 2007, IKEA, a Swedish furniture chain, sold over one million kitchens worldwide. The average budget for a \"major\" kitchen overhaul in 2006, calculates Remodeling magazine, was a staggering $54,000; even a \"minor\" improvement cost on average $18,000.\nExclusivity, more familiar in the world of high fashion, has reached the kitchen: Robinson & Cornish, a British manufacturer of custom-made kitchens, offers a Georgian-style one which would cost 145,000-155,000—excluding building, plumbing and electrical work. Its big selling point is that nobody else will have it: \"You won't see this kitchen anywhere else in the world.\"\nThe elevation of the room that once belonged only to the servants to that of design showcase for the modern family tells the story of a century of social change. Right into the early 20th century, kitchens were smoky, noisy places, generally located underground, or to the back of the house, and as far from living space as possible. That was as it should be: kitchens were for servants, and the aspiring middle classes wanted nothing to do with them.\nBut as the working classes prospered and the servant shortage set in, housekeeping became a matter of interest to the educated classes. One of the pioneers of a radical new way of thinking about the kitchen was Catharine Esther Beecher, sister of Harriet Beecher Stowe. In American Woman's Home, published in 1869, the Beecher sisters recommended a scientific approach to household management, designed to enhance the efficiency of a woman's work and promote order.\nMany contemporary ideas about kitchen design can be traced back to another American, Christine Frederick, who set about enhancing the efficiency of the housewife. Her 1919 work, Household Engineering: Scientific Management in the Home, was based on detailed observation of a housewife's daily routine. She borrowed the principle of efficiency on the factory floor and applied it to domestic tasks on the kitchen floor.\nFrederick's central idea, that \"stove, sink and kitchen table must be placed in such a relation that useless steps are avoided entirely\", inspired the first fully fitted kitchen, designed in the 1920s by Margarete Schütte-Lihotsky. It was a modernist triumph, and many elements remain central features of today's kitchen.", "53": "These days, nobody needs to cook. Families graze on high-cholesterol take-aways and microwaved ready-meals. Cooking is an occasional hobby and a vehicle for celebrity chefs. Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern house: what the great hall was to the medieval castle, the kitchen is to the 21st-century home.\nThe money spent on kitchens has risen with their status. In America the kitchen market is now worth $170 billion, five times the country's film industry. In the year to August 2007, IKEA, a Swedish furniture chain, sold over one million kitchens worldwide. The average budget for a \"major\" kitchen overhaul in 2006, calculates Remodeling magazine, was a staggering $54,000; even a \"minor\" improvement cost on average $18,000.\nExclusivity, more familiar in the world of high fashion, has reached the kitchen: Robinson & Cornish, a British manufacturer of custom-made kitchens, offers a Georgian-style one which would cost 145,000-155,000—excluding building, plumbing and electrical work. Its big selling point is that nobody else will have it: \"You won't see this kitchen anywhere else in the world.\"\nThe elevation of the room that once belonged only to the servants to that of design showcase for the modern family tells the story of a century of social change. Right into the early 20th century, kitchens were smoky, noisy places, generally located underground, or to the back of the house, and as far from living space as possible. That was as it should be: kitchens were for servants, and the aspiring middle classes wanted nothing to do with them.\nBut as the working classes prospered and the servant shortage set in, housekeeping became a matter of interest to the educated classes. One of the pioneers of a radical new way of thinking about the kitchen was Catharine Esther Beecher, sister of Harriet Beecher Stowe. In American Woman's Home, published in 1869, the Beecher sisters recommended a scientific approach to household management, designed to enhance the efficiency of a woman's work and promote order.\nMany contemporary ideas about kitchen design can be traced back to another American, Christine Frederick, who set about enhancing the efficiency of the housewife. Her 1919 work, Household Engineering: Scientific Management in the Home, was based on detailed observation of a housewife's daily routine. She borrowed the principle of efficiency on the factory floor and applied it to domestic tasks on the kitchen floor.\nFrederick's central idea, that \"stove, sink and kitchen table must be placed in such a relation that useless steps are avoided entirely\", inspired the first fully fitted kitchen, designed in the 1920s by Margarete Schütte-Lihotsky. It was a modernist triumph, and many elements remain central features of today's kitchen.", "54": "These days, nobody needs to cook. Families graze on high-cholesterol take-aways and microwaved ready-meals. Cooking is an occasional hobby and a vehicle for celebrity chefs. Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern house: what the great hall was to the medieval castle, the kitchen is to the 21st-century home.\nThe money spent on kitchens has risen with their status. In America the kitchen market is now worth $170 billion, five times the country's film industry. In the year to August 2007, IKEA, a Swedish furniture chain, sold over one million kitchens worldwide. The average budget for a \"major\" kitchen overhaul in 2006, calculates Remodeling magazine, was a staggering $54,000; even a \"minor\" improvement cost on average $18,000.\nExclusivity, more familiar in the world of high fashion, has reached the kitchen: Robinson & Cornish, a British manufacturer of custom-made kitchens, offers a Georgian-style one which would cost 145,000-155,000—excluding building, plumbing and electrical work. Its big selling point is that nobody else will have it: \"You won't see this kitchen anywhere else in the world.\"\nThe elevation of the room that once belonged only to the servants to that of design showcase for the modern family tells the story of a century of social change. Right into the early 20th century, kitchens were smoky, noisy places, generally located underground, or to the back of the house, and as far from living space as possible. That was as it should be: kitchens were for servants, and the aspiring middle classes wanted nothing to do with them.\nBut as the working classes prospered and the servant shortage set in, housekeeping became a matter of interest to the educated classes. One of the pioneers of a radical new way of thinking about the kitchen was Catharine Esther Beecher, sister of Harriet Beecher Stowe. In American Woman's Home, published in 1869, the Beecher sisters recommended a scientific approach to household management, designed to enhance the efficiency of a woman's work and promote order.\nMany contemporary ideas about kitchen design can be traced back to another American, Christine Frederick, who set about enhancing the efficiency of the housewife. Her 1919 work, Household Engineering: Scientific Management in the Home, was based on detailed observation of a housewife's daily routine. She borrowed the principle of efficiency on the factory floor and applied it to domestic tasks on the kitchen floor.\nFrederick's central idea, that \"stove, sink and kitchen table must be placed in such a relation that useless steps are avoided entirely\", inspired the first fully fitted kitchen, designed in the 1920s by Margarete Schütte-Lihotsky. It was a modernist triumph, and many elements remain central features of today's kitchen.", "55": "These days, nobody needs to cook. Families graze on high-cholesterol take-aways and microwaved ready-meals. Cooking is an occasional hobby and a vehicle for celebrity chefs. Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern house: what the great hall was to the medieval castle, the kitchen is to the 21st-century home.\nThe money spent on kitchens has risen with their status. In America the kitchen market is now worth $170 billion, five times the country's film industry. In the year to August 2007, IKEA, a Swedish furniture chain, sold over one million kitchens worldwide. The average budget for a \"major\" kitchen overhaul in 2006, calculates Remodeling magazine, was a staggering $54,000; even a \"minor\" improvement cost on average $18,000.\nExclusivity, more familiar in the world of high fashion, has reached the kitchen: Robinson & Cornish, a British manufacturer of custom-made kitchens, offers a Georgian-style one which would cost 145,000-155,000—excluding building, plumbing and electrical work. Its big selling point is that nobody else will have it: \"You won't see this kitchen anywhere else in the world.\"\nThe elevation of the room that once belonged only to the servants to that of design showcase for the modern family tells the story of a century of social change. Right into the early 20th century, kitchens were smoky, noisy places, generally located underground, or to the back of the house, and as far from living space as possible. That was as it should be: kitchens were for servants, and the aspiring middle classes wanted nothing to do with them.\nBut as the working classes prospered and the servant shortage set in, housekeeping became a matter of interest to the educated classes. One of the pioneers of a radical new way of thinking about the kitchen was Catharine Esther Beecher, sister of Harriet Beecher Stowe. In American Woman's Home, published in 1869, the Beecher sisters recommended a scientific approach to household management, designed to enhance the efficiency of a woman's work and promote order.\nMany contemporary ideas about kitchen design can be traced back to another American, Christine Frederick, who set about enhancing the efficiency of the housewife. Her 1919 work, Household Engineering: Scientific Management in the Home, was based on detailed observation of a housewife's daily routine. She borrowed the principle of efficiency on the factory floor and applied it to domestic tasks on the kitchen floor.\nFrederick's central idea, that \"stove, sink and kitchen table must be placed in such a relation that useless steps are avoided entirely\", inspired the first fully fitted kitchen, designed in the 1920s by Margarete Schütte-Lihotsky. It was a modernist triumph, and many elements remain central features of today's kitchen."}, "questions": [{"id": "2016-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) arises\n动词\n【词性槽】the question 作主语，缺谓语动词，单数第三人称一般现在时\n【依据】the question arises 是「问题出现」的固定表达\nthe question naturally arises: Who will be responsible when they do something\nwrong? 中 arises 三单形式恰好匹配 the question 单数主语，「问题自然产生」也直接引\n出下文那三连问。\n【竞争词】B) ascends ascends 指「上升、登顶」，不与抽象 question 搭配\nascend 多用于物理攀升或地位上升(ascend the throne / ascend to power)，「问题上升」不\n是正常英语表达，且与「自然引出疑问」的语境无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the question 作主语，缺谓语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) combination\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 The 后、of 短语前是名词作主语\n【依据】and 把两个力量拼在一起 → 组合\nof 后是 computational power and engineering advances 两个东西，与「组合」语义天\n然契合，且单数与 The 一致；它「将带来低成本居家护理」也只有「两股力量的结合」逻\n辑顺。\n【竞争词】J) penalties 复数 penalties 与单数动词 will enable 主谓不一致，且语义反向\npenalties 是复数(就形态而言句子也能走，但本质偏离句意)；其语义「惩罚」与「带来低成本居\n家护理」完全冲突，无法主语化这一积极后果。\nN) victims 「受害者的…」完全不匹配后文积极语义\nvictims 指「受害者」，与「促成无障碍居家护理、无人驾驶」的正面后果不搭，也不与「计算\n力 + 工程进展」形成自然属格。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定冠词 The 后、of 短语前是名词作"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) eventually\n副词\n【词性槽】情态动词 will 与实义动词 enable 之间，缺一个修饰动词的副词\n【依据】对应未来时 will，强调技术演进的最终结果\n句子用 will __(28)__ enable，且整段在展望 In the future, they will improve our health\ncare，「最终能够实现」语义与 eventually(最终) 完美契合。\n【竞争词】M) proximately 词义指「邻近地/直接地」，与「未来终将」的时间感反向\nproximately 表示「在原因链上直接地」或「在空间/时间上邻近地」，放进 will __(28)__\nenable 不仅与 will 表达的未来感冲突，还和后文 In the future 的远景描写矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 will 与实义动词 enab"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) widespread\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 use 前需一个定语形容词\n【依据】「无人驾驶车的普及使用」才能减少酒驾分心事故\n__(29)__ use of driverless cars that may reduce drunk- and distracted-driving\naccidents 强调通过「广泛使用」无人车来减少事故，widespread use 是英语固定搭配，\n正中此意。\n【竞争词】E) definite definite use 不是英语习惯搭配，语义也只表「明确的使用」\ndefinite 强调「明确的、不可置疑的」，无法解释「为何能减少酒驾事故」——减少事故靠的是\n规模化普及，不是「明确」。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 use 前需一个定语形容词"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) bound\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词与 to do 之间是表语形容词位\n【依据】be bound to do 是「必然/注定要…」的固定搭配\nthere are __(30)__ to be problems. 等于 there are bound to be problems，意为「必然\n会有问题」。下一句立刻列出种种事故 Robot cars will crash. A drone(遥控飞行器)\noperator will __(31)__ someone's privacy.，印证这种「注定」。\n【竞争词】E) definite definite 不与 to do 直接搭配\ndefinite 表「明确的」，不存在 be definite to do 的固定结构，且语义「明确会有问题」也不\n如「注定会有问题」贴合后文的事故罗列。\nO) widespread 义为「广泛的」，与 there be…to do 句型不兼容\nwidespread 修饰名词或作表语描述分布范围，不能后接 to do 不定式表「注定要…」；语义也\n偏离段意。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— 「definite to be problems」看似也成立，实则无此搭配。\n考生若不知道 be bound to do 这个固定结构，会被 definite「明确的」诱惑——「明确会\n有问题」中文听着也通。防坑要点：英语里能进 be ___ to do 的形容词是有限的\n(bound/likely/sure/about/certain)，definite 不在其中。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词与 to do 之间是表语形容"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) invade\n动词\n【词性槽】助动词 will 后接动词原形，且后跟宾语 someone's privacy\n【依据】invade one's privacy 是「侵犯隐私」的固定搭配\nwill __(31)__ someone's privacy 与 invade privacy 完美对应——这是英语高频法律/新闻\n搭配，对应中文「侵犯隐私」。\n【竞争词】G) interfere interfere 是不及物动词，必须带 with，不能直接接 someone's privacy\ninterfere 后需 with 才能接宾语(interfere with one's privacy)，原句中没有 with，语法上直接\n排除；同时 invade privacy 是更地道的搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— interfere 义近 invade 但不及物。interfere 在中文翻译里\n也常被译成「干涉、侵犯」，看着和 invade 同义。防坑要点：及物性是动词位的硬约束，\n没有 with 不能选 interfere；invade privacy 是英语写作高频固定搭配，认死了即可。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·助动词 will 后接动词原形，且后跟宾"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) victims\n名词\n【词性槽】the ___ of machines 是名词位\n【依据】「机器的受害者」获得陪审团同情\nJuries sympathetic to the victims of machines——陪审团同情的对象自然是被机器伤害\n的「受害者」，与下文 punish entrepreneurs 的因果一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the ___ of machines"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) penalties\n名词\n【词性槽】with 后、and damages 并列前是名词位\n【依据】「陪审团惩罚企业家」的工具是处罚 + 赔偿\nJuries sympathetic to the __(32)__ of machines will punish entrepreneurs with\ncompany-crushing __(33)__ and damages. 里 punish 提示惩罚手段；penalties(罚款) 与\ndamages(损害赔偿) 是法律语境中典型的双驾马车。\n【竞争词】N) victims victims 是人，无法作「惩罚」的工具/手段\nwith victims and damages 在语义上不成立——受害者不是惩罚企业家的「工具」。同时 N 词\n更适合留给 32 题「机器的 ___」那个名词位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·with 后、and damages 并"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) preserving\n动词\n【词性槽】while 后缺一个动名词作伴随状语\n【依据】与 protect 形成「保护民众 / 保留创新空间」的并列对照\nto protect people while __(34)__ space for innovation 是经典的 while 对照结构：前半\n保护民众、后半保留创新余地。preserve space 是「保留空间」的固定搭配，逻辑顺。\n【竞争词】I) manifesting 义为「显现、表明」，无法和 space 构成「留出空间」语义\nmanifest space 不是固定搭配；且文段在讨论「如何既保护人又给创新留空间」，需要的是\n「保留」，不是「显现」。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·while 后缺一个动名词作伴随状语"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arises", "B) ascends", "C) bound", "D) combination", "E) definite", "F) eventually", "G) interfere", "H) invade", "I) manifesting", "J) penalties", "K) preserving", "L) programmed", "M) proximately", "N) victims", "O) widespread"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) programmed\n动词\n【词性槽】并列结构 built, ___ and sold 中缺一个过去分词\n【依据】与 built/sold 构成「造 → 编程 → 卖」的完整生产链\n无人车这类机器人系统在 driverless cars, should be built, __(35)__ and sold by\nmanufacturers 的语境里，先组装、再编程、再出售，programmed 正中产品生命周期\n叙述。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列结构 built, ___ and"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "With a tax imposed on expensive health insurance plans, most employers will likely transfer money from health expenses into wages.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「对昂贵保险计划征税 + 雇主把钱从医疗福利转入工资」双锚点\n【定位】Economists project that most employers would shift money from expensive health benefits\ninto wages.\n经济学家预测，大多数雇主会把钱从昂贵的健康福利转移到工资中。\n【改写】a tax imposed on expensive health insurance plans ↔\nan excise tax(消费税) on health insurance plans that cost more than $8,000 for an individual or\n$21,000 for a family\n「税 + 昂贵保险计划」对应\nmost employers will likely transfer money from health expenses into wages ↔\nmost employers would shift money from expensive health benefits into wages\n「同义动词 + 同指名词」逐词对照\n【辨邻】 E 本段虽谈节省成本，但机制是reduce the rate of growth in annual Medicare\npayments to hospitals——削减 Medicare 给医院的付款增速，与「对昂贵保险计划\n征税 + 雇主把钱挪到工资」毫无对应；且本段主体是 Medicare 与提供方，不是雇主\n与员工工资。\nBoth bills would reduce the rate of growth in annual Medicare payments to hospitals, nursing\nhomes and other providers by amounts comparable to the productivity savings routinely made in\nother industries", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Changes in policy would be approved or rejected as a whole so that lobbyists would find it hard to influence lawmakers.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「政策变更整体表决 + 游说者难影响立法者」双锚点\n【定位】The changes would have to be approved or rejected as a whole by Congress, making it hard\nfor narrow-interest lobbies to bend lawmakers to their will.\n这些变更必须由国会整体批准或否决，使得狭隘利益的游说集团难以让立法者按其意志\n行事。\n【改写】Changes in policy would be approved or rejected as a whole ↔\nThe changes would have to be approved or rejected as a whole by Congress\n核心程序短语原样复现\nlobbyists would find it hard to influence lawmakers ↔\nmaking it hard for narrow-interest lobbies to bend lawmakers to their will\n「lobbyists/lobbies」同源 +「influence/bend…to their will」近义\n【辨邻】 E 本段确实出现了游说话题，但讲的是国会在被游说时会give in to lobbyists and let\ninefficient providers off the hook(放过)——也就是「国会被说服后放过低效提供\n方」；而陈述句要的是「整体表决程序使游说难以奏效」，机制是程序设计而非妥协警\n告，故本段不是源头段。\nCritics say Congress will give in to lobbyists and let inefficient providers off the hook(放过)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is not easy to curb the rising medical costs in America.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】「难以遏制美国医疗成本上涨」总论判断\n【定位】The fundamental fix—reshaping how care is delivered and how doctors are paid in a\nwasteful, abnormal system—is likely to be achieved only through trial and error and\nincremental(渐进的) gains.\n根本性修复——重新塑造一个浪费而反常体系中医疗服务的提供方式和医生的报酬方式——\n很可能只有通过反复试错和渐进式收益才能实现。\n【改写】It is not easy to curb the rising medical costs in America ↔\nno one has an easy fix for rising medical costs\n「难治」态度词的同义改写\n【辨邻】 B 本段The good news is that a bill just approved by the House and a bill\napproved by the Senate Finance Committee would implement or test many\nreforms that should help slow the rise in medical costs over the long term强调\n法案带来的乐观前景，是「能减缓」的正面态度；陈述句要的是「不易遏制」的负面态\n度，方向相反。\nThe good news is that a bill just approved by the House and a bill approved by the Senate Finance\nCommittee would implement or test many reforms that should help slow the rise in medical costs\nover the long term\nC 本段Medical spending, which typically rises faster than wages and the overall\neconomy, is propelled by two things只在分析「被什么推动」，并未就「能否遏\n制」表明态度；陈述句要的是「不易遏制」这一态度判定，本段不承载该判定。\nMedical spending, which typically rises faster than wages and the overall economy, is propelled by\ntwo things: the high prices charged for medical services in this country and the volume of\nunnecessary care delivered by doctors and hospitals", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Standardization of forms for automatic processing will save a lot of medical expenses.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「表单标准化 + 自动化处理 + 省钱」三重锚点\n【定位】When the health insurance industry was still cooperating in reform efforts, its trade group\noffered to provide standardized forms for automated processing.\n在健康保险业还配合改革之时，其行业协会曾提议提供标准化表单用于自动化处理。\n【改写】Standardization of forms for automatic processing ↔\nstandardized forms for automated processing\n同根词词形互换\nsave a lot of medical expenses ↔ save hundreds of billions of dollars over the next decade\n「概括省钱」对应「具体省多少钱」", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Republicans and the insurance industry are strongly opposed to the creation of a public insurance plan.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「共和党人 + 保险业 + 反对公共保险计划」三重锚点\n【定位】The final legislation might throw a public plan into the competition, but thanks to the fierce\nopposition of the insurance industry and Republican critics, it might not save much money.\n最终立法或许会把一项公共计划投入竞争，但由于保险业和共和党批评者的强烈反对，它可\n能省不下多少钱。\n【改写】Republicans and the insurance industry are strongly opposed ↔\nthe fierce opposition of the insurance industry and Republican critics\n动词 strongly opposed 与名词 fierce opposition 等值\nthe creation of a public insurance plan ↔ a public plan\n概括上位词 public plan 对应 public insurance plan\n【辨邻】 K 本段small businesses and individuals could choose from an array of private\nplans and possibly a public option只是说交易所里可能有公共选项，并未陈述谁反\n对公共计划，与陈述句的「Republicans 与保险业反对」无对应。\nsmall businesses and individuals could choose from an array of private plans and possibly a\npublic option", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Conversion of paper to electronic medical records will help eliminate redundant tests and prevent drug interactions.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「纸质转电子病历 + 消除冗余检查 + 防止药物相互作用」三重锚点\n【定位】In time it should help restrain costs by eliminating redundant tests, preventing drug\ninteractions, and helping doctors find the best treatments.\n假以时日，它应能通过消除冗余检查、预防药物相互作用，以及帮助医生找到最佳治疗方案\n来抑制成本。\n【改写】Conversion of paper to electronic medical records ↔\nconvert the inefficient, paper-driven medical system to electronic records\n名词化 conversion ↔ 动词 convert\neliminate redundant tests ↔ eliminating redundant tests\n动词形态切换（-ing ↔ 原型）\nprevent drug interactions ↔ preventing drug interactions\n动词形态切换（-ing ↔ 原型）", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The high cost of medical services and unnecessary tests and treatments have driven up medical expenses.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「医疗服务高价 + 不必要的检查与治疗」双因锚点\n【定位】Medical spending, which typically rises faster than wages and the overall economy, is\npropelled by two things: the high prices charged for medical services in this country and the\nvolume of unnecessary care delivered by doctors and hospitals, which often perform a lot\nmore tests and treatments than a patient really needs.\n医疗开支通常涨得比工资和整体经济还快，它由两件事推动：本国医疗服务收取的高价格，\n以及医生和医院提供的不必要诊疗量——它们经常做远超患者真正所需的检查与治疗。\n【改写】The high cost of medical services ↔\nthe high prices charged for medical services in this country\ncost ↔ prices 同指 +「医疗服务」原样\nunnecessary tests and treatments ↔\nthe volume of unnecessary care delivered by doctors and hospitals, which often perform a lot\nmore tests and treatments than a patient really needs\n「不必要的检查与治疗」原样落地\ndriven up medical expenses ↔ Medical spending … is propelled by two things\n推动类动词同义 +「医疗开支」上位词对应\n【辨邻】 I 本段the fee-for-service system…is a primary reason that the cost of care is so\nhigh只给出一项制度性根因（按服务量付费），与陈述句的「高价 + 不必要诊疗」两\n项并列结构不符。\nthe fee-for-service system—doctors are rewarded for the quantity of care rather than its quality or\neffectiveness—is a primary reason that the cost of care is so high", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "One main factor that has driven up medical expenses is that doctors are compensated for the amount of care rather than its effect.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「医生按数量而非效果获报」的根因判断\n【定位】Virtually all experts agree that the fee-for-service system—doctors are rewarded for the\nquantity of care rather than its quality or effectiveness—is a primary reason that the cost of\ncare is so high.\n几乎所有专家都同意：按服务量付费的制度——医生按其医疗服务的数量而非质量或效果获\n报——是医疗成本如此之高的一项主要原因。\n【改写】doctors are compensated for the amount of care rather than its effect ↔\ndoctors are rewarded for the quantity of care rather than its quality or effectiveness\n对照框架原样 + 三对同义词\nOne main factor that has driven up medical expenses ↔\nis a primary reason that the cost of care is so high\n「主因 + 推高开支」同义改写\n【辨邻】 C 本段the high prices charged for medical services in this country and the volume\nof unnecessary care delivered by doctors and hospitals给的两项原因都不涉及\n「按服务量而非质量/效果付费」这一独特对照，与陈述句的「rather than effect」对\n照结构不符。\nthe high prices charged for medical services in this country and the volume of unnecessary care\ndelivered by doctors and hospitals", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Contrary to analysts' doubts, the author believes drug prices may be lowered through negotiation.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「分析师存疑 vs 作者相信谈判可降药价」观点反差锚点\n【定位】Some authoritative analysts doubt that the secretary would get better deals than private\ninsurers already get. We believe negotiation could work.\n一些权威分析人士怀疑卫生部长能否谈到比私营保险公司已经拿到的更好的价；我们相信谈\n判是可行的。\n【改写】Contrary to analysts' doubts ↔\nSome authoritative analysts doubt that the secretary would get better deals than private\ninsurers already get\n名词化 doubts 对应动词 doubt 的并列前句\nthe author believes drug prices may be lowered through negotiation ↔\nWe believe negotiation could work\n「We → the author」人称转换 +「negotiation could work → 谈判可降药价」语境补完\n【辨邻】 O 本段Congress needs to find the courage to allow Medicare to pay preferentially\nfor treatments proven to be superior主张按疗效优劣付款，并未提及「药价谈判」\n这一独特机制；与陈述句的「谈判降药价」无对应。\nCongress needs to find the courage to allow Medicare to pay preferentially for treatments proven to\nbe superior", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Fair competition might create a strong incentive for insurers to charge less.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「公平竞争催生保险公司降价激励」机制锚点\n【定位】To get access to millions of new customers, insurers would have a strong incentive to\nlower their prices, perhaps by accepting slimmer profit margins or demanding better deals\nfrom providers.\n为打入数以百万计的新客户，保险公司将有强烈动机降价，办法可能是接受更薄的利润率或\n向提供方索要更好的条件。\n【改写】a strong incentive for insurers to charge less ↔\ninsurers would have a strong incentive to lower their prices\n「strong incentive」原样 +「charge less」与「lower their prices」同义\nFair competition ↔\nhealth insurance exchanges on which small businesses and individuals could choose from an\narray of private plans and possibly a public option. All the plans would have to provide\nstandard benefit packages that would be easy to compare\n抽象概念「公平竞争」对应具体制度「交易所 + 标准化 + 可选」\n【辨邻】 L 本段it might not save much money给出反向结论；且全段讨论的是「公共计划本身\n能不能省钱」，而非「公平竞争催生私营保险公司降价激励」这一机制；与陈述句的正\n向机制不符。\nThe final legislation might throw a public plan into the competition, but thanks to the fierce\nopposition of the insurance industry and Republican critics, it might not save much money\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n开篇定调：美国人忧心医疗成本与保费持续上涨，问改革能否解决；作者直接给出态\n度——没人有简单办法，no one has an easy fix for rising medical costs，根本性修复\n要靠trial and error and incremental(渐进的) gains反复试错和渐进推进。\n信息指纹 「没有简单办法 + 试错渐进」开宗明义\nB 好消息：众议院通过的法案和参议院财政委员会通过的法案会推行或试行许多改革，\n有望长期减缓医疗成本上涨；并引《新英格兰医学杂志》评价Pretty much every\nproposed innovation found in the health policy literature these days is contained in\nthese measures。\n信息指纹 「两院法案 + NEJM 评语」总括\nC 诊断医疗开支为何上涨：受两件事推动——the high prices charged for medical\nservices in this country高昂价格，以及the volume of unnecessary care delivered by\ndoctors and hospitals医生与医院提供的不必要诊疗量，常常做远多于患者真正需要的\n检查与治疗。\n信息指纹 「高价 + 不必要诊疗」双因驱动\nD\n承上启下的过渡段：宣布下面将给出两院法案里针对上述问题的若干重要提议，以及\n为什么很难预判它们效果好坏。\n信息指纹 「过渡承启」无强指纹\nE\n两院法案都会按其他行业的生产率节余幅度，削减 Medicare 对医院、养老院等提供\n方的年付款增速；估计十年可省more than $100 billion；并警告国会可能give in to\nlobbyists and let inefficient providers off the hook(放过)，应配pay-go规则约束。\n信息指纹 「Medicare 生产率节余 + 千亿美元 + pay-go」\nF\n参议院财政法案拟对超过$8,000 for an individual or $21,000 for a family的昂贵健康保\n险计划征消费税；保险公司会重设计划压到门槛之下，参保人自付增加从而三思于昂\n贵或冗余的检查；经济学家预测most employers would shift money from expensive\nhealth benefits into wages。\n信息指纹 「8000/21000 美元门槛消费税 + 福利转工资」\nG 简化必能省钱：医生与患者都被多家保险公司各异的表格折磨；保险业行业协会曾承\n诺提供标准化表单以便自动化处理，估计save hundreds of billions of dollars over the\nnext decade；法案会把这一承诺写进法律。\n信息指纹 「表格标准化 + 自动化处理」独有承诺\nH 刺激计划拨款把低效的纸质医疗体系转为电子病历，便于查阅传输；最终将通过\neliminating redundant tests, preventing drug interactions, and helping doctors find\nthe best treatments来约束成本。\n信息指纹 「纸质转电子病历」独有方案\nI\n几乎所有专家都同意the fee-for-service system—doctors are rewarded for the\nquantity of care rather than its quality or effectiveness是看病贵的主要原因；解法是\n推动医生接受按病或按年人头的固定付费；法案在 Medicare 内启动试点，包括\naccountable care organizations与chronic disease management。\n信息指纹 「按服务量付费 vs 质量效果」根因\nJ 参议院法案将设立独立委员会监督试点并建议 Medicare 付款政策调整；变更须由国\n会整体批准或否决，使得making it hard for narrow-interest lobbies to bend\nlawmakers to their will，狭隘游说集团难以左右议员。\n信息指纹 「政策整体表决 + 阻击游说」\nK\n两院法案都将建立健康保险交易所，让小企业与个人从一系列私营计划中挑选，并可\n能含一项公共选项；所有计划须提供标准福利包便于比较；为获得新客户，保险公司\n将有a strong incentive to lower their prices强动机降价。\n信息指纹 「保险交易所 + 降价激励」\nL\n最终立法或许会在竞争中加入公共计划，但由于the fierce opposition of the insurance\nindustry and Republican critics保险业与共和党批评者的强烈反对，可能省不下太多\n钱；众议院法案版本须与提供方协商费率而非套用 Medicare 费率。\n信息指纹 「保险业 + 共和党反对公共计划」\nM 总统刺激计划拨款研究各种治疗方式的疗效比较：前列腺癌选手术、放疗还是密切监\n测？最新最贵的降胆固醇药是否真的比常见竞争者好？待审法案将再投钱加速这项\n工作。\n信息指纹 「治疗方案疗效比较研究」\nN 批评者称这种「比较疗效」想法会导致医疗配给；作者反驳——只有当你认为患者有\n权获取被证次优的治疗时才成立；结果法案没有像应当做的那样要求把这些研究结果\n用于设定 Medicare 付款标准。\n信息指纹 「配给指控 + 反驳」\nO\n国会需有勇气让 Medicare 优先支付被证更优的治疗；有时最佳治疗也最贵，但总体\n上可借此消除大量不必要乃至危险的检查与治疗，从而压低开支。\n信息指纹 「Medicare 优先支付更优疗法」主张\nP\n众议院法案授权卫生与公众服务部长在 Medicare 与 Medicaid 中谈判药价；权威分析\n人士怀疑部长能否谈到比私营保险公司更好的价；作者立场We believe negotiation\ncould work——别国行得通，相信谈判能成。\n信息指纹 「药价谈判 + 分析师存疑 vs 作者相信」\nQ\n法案缺了认真遏制医疗事故诉讼成本的努力；事故赔偿确实推高高风险专科医生的保\n费，且有证据显示医生会做defensive medicine防御性医疗——为防被告而多做检查与\n治疗。\n信息指纹 「医疗事故诉讼 + 防御性医疗」", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say about the use of raw sewage for farming?", "options": ["A) Its risks cannot be overestimated.", "B) It should be forbidden altogether.", "C) Its benefits outweigh the hazards involved.", "D) It is polluting millions of acres of cropland."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the use of raw sewage for farming? 是问作者对一件\n具体事的看法，在原文有明确表态句可对应，仍属可定位的细节题，而非需要串全文的主\n旨题。\n【拆句】题干 Q46 是一个标准的 what 引导的特殊疑问句，主干是 what does the author say，介\n词短语 about the use of raw sewage for farming 限定 say 的对象——把 raw sewage\n用于 farming 这件事。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】While the practice carries serious health risks for many, those dangers are outweighed by the\nsocial and economic gains for poor urban farmers and consumers who need affordable food.\n尽管这种做法对许多人构成严重健康风险，但这些危险被贫困城市农民及需要廉价食物的消\n费者所获得的社会与经济收益所抵消（即收益大于风险）。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIts risks cannot be overestimated. 是 '风险无论怎么估都不为过、要高度警惕'之意；\n而原文 those dangers are outweighed 恰恰承认风险但认为被收益盖过，与之相反。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt should be forbidden altogether. 是'应一律禁止'，但作者在 P2 明确肯定其综合收\n益，Overly strict standards often fail 还反对一刀切，禁止说法与全文立场相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthose dangers are outweighed by the social and economic gains ↔\nIts benefits outweigh the hazards involved.\n把 P2 中的被动改成主动，再做两组同义替换，就得到 Its benefits outweigh the\nhazards involved.，与预判'收益大于风险'完全等价。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt is polluting millions of acres of cropland. 把 irrigate and fertilize nearly 49\nmillion acres of cropland 中的'灌溉/施肥'偷换成'polluting'，把使用面积包装成污染\n面积。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the main problem caused by the use of wastewater for irrigation?", "options": ["A) Rivers and lakes nearby will gradually become contaminated.", "B) It will drive producers of chemical fertilizers out of business.", "C) Farmers and consumers may be affected by harmful bacteria.", "D) It will make the farm produce less competitive on the market."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the main problem caused by 指向一个具体后果，原文必有句子直接描述这个后\n果，属于可定位的细节题。\n【拆句】题干 Q47 主干为 What is the main problem，过去分词短语 caused by the use of\nwastewater for irrigation 后置修饰 problem，说明问题的起因是'用污水灌溉'。注意\ncaused by 是被动来源，不是 caused，不要错读成'问题导致灌溉'。\n【定位】When this water is used for agricultural irrigation, farmers risk absorbing disease-causing\nbacteria, as do consumers who eat the produce raw and unwashed.\n当这种水被用于农业灌溉时，农民面临吸收致病细菌的风险，生吃且未清洗农产品的消费者\n也同样如此。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 rivers effectively become sewers 说明的是河流先被污染才被农民取用；选项\nRivers and lakes nearby will gradually become contaminated. 反把'灌溉'当成污染\n河流的原因，因果反转。\nB) 过度推断\nIt will drive producers of chemical fertilizers out of business. 说污水灌溉会把化肥\n厂挤出市场——原文只有 expensive chemical fertilizer use can be avoided 说可避免\n使用昂贵化肥，并未提及化肥生产商倒闭。\nC) ✓ 正确\nfarmers risk absorbing disease-causing bacteria, as do consumers ↔\nFarmers and consumers may be affected by harmful bacteria.\n并列主语 farmers and consumers 对应、disease-causing bacteria 对应 harmful\nbacteria，预判中'两类人都中招细菌'与 C 完全等价。\nD) 无中生有\nIt will make the farm produce less competitive on the market. 说会让农产品市场竞\n争力下降，原文从未讨论农产品的市场竞争问题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is environmental scientist Pay Drechsel's attitude towards the use of untreated human waste in agriculture?", "options": ["A) Favorable.", "B) Skeptical.", "C) Indifferent.", "D) Responsible."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】What is environmental scientist Pay Drechsel's attitude towards 中 attitude towards\n是态度题的标志性结构。\n【拆句】题干 Q48 主干是 What is ... attitude，名词短语 environmental scientist Pay Drechsel's\n是 attitude 的所有格修饰，介词短语 towards the use of untreated human waste in\nagriculture 指明态度的对象。结构清晰。\n【定位】Those dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free\nwater and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to\nescape poverty.\n他说，那些危险可以通过对农民和消费者的教育加以解决，与此同时，来自人类废物的免费\n水和养分可帮助发展中国家的城市农民摆脱贫困。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe social and economic benefits of using untreated human waste to grow food outweigh the\nhealth risks\n↔ Favorable.\ncan help urban farmers in developing countries to escape poverty ↔ Favorable.\nP5 P6 中 Drechsel 的全部话语只出现 benefits、outweigh、help、escape 等正面动\n词，没有任何 reservation/however 等保留信号，态度收敛为 Favorable.。\nB) 无中生有\nSkeptical. 需要原文出现 doubt/question/uncertain/may not 等怀疑信号，P5 P6 全\n是正面陈述，无此类信号。\nC) 与原文相反\nIndifferent. 是'漠不关心'，但 Drechsel 主动 argues、积极给出解决方案，绝非旁\n观者。\nD) 答非所问\nResponsible. 是'负责任'，描述人的品格而非对某事的态度极性；态度题答案应是\nfavorable/critical/neutral 一类'方向词'，responsible 不在态度词谱系内。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Pay Dreschsel think of the risks involved in using untreated human waste for farming?", "options": ["A) They have been somewhat exaggerated.", "B) They can be dealt with through education.", "C) They will be minimized with new technology.", "D) They can be addressed by improved sanitation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does Pay Dreschsel think of the risks 问的是对一个具体对象（风险）的具体看\n法，原文有明确句对应，属可定位的细节题。\n【拆句】题干 Q49 主干 What does Pay Dreschsel think of，介词 of 的宾语是 the risks involved\nin using untreated human waste for farming，其中 involved in 是过去分词短语后置修\n饰 risks，意为'涉及到……的风险'。结构略长但无歧义。\n【定位】Those dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education, he said, while the free\nwater and nutrients from human waste can help urban farmers in developing countries to\nescape poverty.\n他说，那些危险可以通过对农民和消费者的教育加以解决，与此同时，来自人类废物的免费\n水和养分可帮助发展中国家的城市农民摆脱贫困。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey have been somewhat exaggerated. 是说风险被夸大，但 But Pay Drechsel ...\nargues that the social and economic benefits ... outweigh the health risks 只承认\n风险存在但收益更大，并未质疑风险描述的真实性。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThose dangers can be addressed with farmer and consumer education ↔\nThey can be dealt with through education.\naddress↔deal with 同义、with↔through 同表方式，再加上代词 Those\ndangers↔They，三组对应使 B 与定位句几乎逐词等值。\nC) 无中生有\nThey will be minimized with new technology. 提到新技术，但 P6 给出的手段是\neducation 而非 technology，原文从无新技术之说。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey can be addressed by improved sanitation. 说靠改善卫生设施解决，lack of\nproper sanitation 只是把 sanitation 当成致病原因之一，并非 Drechsel 给出的解决手\n段；他的方案是 education。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do we learn about James Bartram's position on the use of human waste for farming?", "options": ["A) He echoes Pay Drechsel's opinion on the issue.", "B) He challenges Liqa Raschid-Sally's conclusion", "C) He thinks it the only way out of the current food crisis.", "D) He deems it indispensable for combating global poverty."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Q50 选项 He echoes Pay Drechsel's opinion on the issue. 和 He challenges Liqa\nRaschid-Sally's conclusion 都涉及与他人的关系，需要把 Bartram 的话与前文人物比对，\n仍是基于定位句的细节题。\n【拆句】题干 Q50 主干 What do we learn about James Bartram's position，介词短语 on the\nuse of human waste for farming 限定 position 的对象。we learn about 是常见的客观\n提问句式，等同于'文章告诉我们'。\n【定位】\"Overly strict standards often fail,\" James Bartram, a WHO water-health expert, said. \"We\nneed to accept that fact across much of the planet, so waste with little or no treatment will be\nused in agriculture for good reason.\"\n\"过于严格的标准往往会失败，\"世界卫生组织水健康专家詹姆斯·巴特拉姆说，\"我们必须接\n受这样一个事实：在地球的很多地方，几乎不经处理或完全不处理的废物将被合理地用于\n农业。\"\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwaste with little or no treatment will be used in agriculture for good reason ↔\nHe echoes Pay Drechsel's opinion on the issue.\nOverly strict standards often fail ↔ He echoes Pay Drechsel's opinion on the issue.\nDrechsel 主张'风险可解、利大于弊、继续使用'，Bartram 主张'严格禁止行不通、可合\n理使用'，方向上一一对应，echoes 是'附和、应和'的最准确表达。\nB) 张冠李戴\nLiqa Raschid-Sally 给出 large potential ... to both help and hurt 这一中立结论；\nBartram 的话并非针对她，且立场也不是反对'有正有负'的结论，谈不上 challenges。\nC) 过度推断\nHe thinks it the only way out of the current food crisis. 把使用污水捧成'解决粮食危\n机的唯一出路'，但 P12 只说在地球很多地方 will be used in agriculture for good\nreason，从未称之为唯一出路，也没出现 food crisis 一词。\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe deems it indispensable for combating global poverty. 中'对抗全球贫困不可或\n缺'是 Drechsel 在 escape poverty 处的论点，而非 Bartram 的——Bartram 全程未\n提 poverty。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about the kitchen of today?", "options": ["A) It is where housewives display their cooking skills.", "B) It is where the family entertains important guests.", "C) It has become something odd in a modern house.", "D) It is regarded as the center of a modern home."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the kitchen of today? 问的是作者就一个具体对象给出\n的具体说法，可在文本中找到直接对应的句子。\n【拆句】题干结构是 What does the author say about the kitchen of today?——一个标准的特殊\n疑问句，谓语 say，宾语就是后面的从句问点，无任何陷阱。\n【定位】Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern house: what the\ngreat hall was to the medieval castle, the kitchen is to the 21st-century home.\n这就使得这件事显得奇怪：厨房竟然成了现代住宅的核心——厨房之于 21 世纪家庭，正如\n大厅之于中世纪城堡。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文反而说 nobody needs to cook，与 housewives display their cooking skills 完\n全相反，文中也未提及任何\"展示技艺\"的功能。\nB) 无中生有\n全文从未把厨房描述为 where the family entertains important guests，作者用的比\n喻是\"家中之心\"而非\"会客场所\"，二者并不等价。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 Which makes it odd that the kitchen has become the heart of the modern\nhouse 中 odd 修饰的是\"没人做饭却把厨房当核心\"这一反差，而非把厨房本身评价为奇\n怪之物；It has become something odd in a modern house. 把 odd 的指向从\"现\n象\"偷换为\"厨房本身\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe kitchen has become the heart of the modern house ↔\nIt is regarded as the center of a modern home.\n\"厨房 = 现代住宅的核心\"与 It is regarded as the center of a modern home. 在词项\n和句法上几乎一对一对应，可直接锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why does the Georgian-style kitchen sell at a very high price?", "options": ["A) It is believed to have tremendous artistic value.", "B) No duplicate is to be found in any other place.", "C) It is manufactured by a famous British company.", "D) No other manufacturer can produce anything like it."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why does the Georgian-style kitchen sell at a very high price? 是 Why 类直接问因果\n的具体性提问，答案在原文中有句对应。\n【拆句】Why does the Georgian-style kitchen sell at a very high price——does 是助动词、主\n语是 the Georgian-style kitchen、谓语 sell 取\"售价为/卖到\"的意义，sell at + 价格是固\n定搭配，无从句、无陷阱。\n【定位】Its big selling point is that nobody else will have it: \"You won\\'t see this kitchen anywhere\nelse in the world.\"\n它最大的卖点在于：再没别人会拥有同款——\"你在世界上别的任何地方都见不到这款\n厨房。\"\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文卖点是\"独一无二\"而非 tremendous artistic value，文中既未出现 artistic 也未出\n现任何评价艺术性的措辞。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnobody else will have it ↔ No duplicate is to be found in any other place.\nYou won\\'t see this kitchen anywhere else in the world. ↔ in any other place\n预判得到的\"独一无二\"被 No duplicate 用否定式同义改写，正是阅读改写的经典手法，\n直接锁定 B。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文只称 Robinson & Cornish, a British manufacturer of custom-made kitchens，\n未称其\"著名\"；高价的原因是\"定制独款\"而不是\"厂家有名\"，把\"独款\"换成\"名厂\"属\n偷换。\nD) 过度推断\n原文 nobody else will have it 强调\"没人会拥有同款\"，是商家承诺的稀缺，而非 No\nother manufacturer can produce anything like it. 所指的\"别家厂没这能力造\"，后者\n是把稀缺性升级为唯一制造能力。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the change in the status of the kitchen reflect?", "options": ["A) Improved living conditions.", "B) Women's elevated status.", "C) Technological progress.", "D) Social change."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the change in the status of the kitchen reflect? 是对一个具体陈述的指向性\n提问，答案在原文有明确等价说法。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does [the change in the status of the kitchen] reflect?——一个标准\n特殊疑问句，方括号内是嵌套了 of 介宾结构的复杂主语，理解时要把 in 和 of 两层都拆出\n来：\"厨房的地位的变化\"。\n【定位】The elevation of the room that once belonged only to the servants to that of design showcase\nfor the modern family tells the story of a century of social change.\n这个曾经只属于仆人的房间，如今升格为现代家庭的设计展示空间，这一升格本身讲述着一\n个世纪以来的社会变迁。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nImproved living conditions. 仅是 social change 在物质层面的一个细分项，原文论断\n指向更宽的社会变迁，不能用一个具体侧面替代整体。\nB) 过度推断\n原文仅说\"工人阶级富裕 + 仆人短缺\"导致家政进入受教育阶层视野，未直接说厨房地位\n变化反映 Women\\'s elevated status；把\"家政成为关注对象\"等同于\"女性地位提升\"是\n引申过头。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文第 7 段提到的全功能厨房只是社会变迁的一个产物，整段论断指向 social change\n而非单纯 Technological progress.，技术进步是结果端的一个具体表现而非\"被反映\"的\n本体。\nD) ✓ 正确\na century of social change ↔ Social change.\ntells the story of ↔ reflect\n预判已锁定\"反映的对象是社会变迁\"，Social change. 是最直接对应的截取式答案。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What was the Beecher sisters' idea of a kitchen?", "options": ["A) A place where women could work more efficiently.", "B) A place where high technology could be applied.", "C) A place of interest to the educated people.", "D) A place to experiment with new ideas."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What was the Beecher sisters' idea of a kitchen? 问的是具体人物的具体观点，文本中必\n有一句直接给出。\n【拆句】What was the Beecher sisters\\' idea of a kitchen——名词 idea 后接 of 介宾，整体\n相当于\"贝彻姐妹关于厨房的主张是什么\"，注意 \\'s 表所有格，主语其实是 idea 而不\n是 sisters。\n【定位】In American Woman\\'s Home, published in 1869, the Beecher sisters recommended a\nscientific approach to household management, designed to enhance the efficiency of a\nwoman\\'s work and promote order.\n在 1869 年出版的《美国妇女之家》中，贝彻姐妹倡导用科学的方法管理家务，旨在提升女\n性劳作的效率并促进家中秩序。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nenhance the efficiency of a woman\\'s work ↔ where women could work more efficiently\nscientific approach to household management ↔\nA place where women could work more efficiently.\n预判\"用科学方法提高效率\"中最贴近\"厨房应当是怎样的场所\"的描述就是\"让女性更高效\n工作的场所\"，正与 A 同义。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文用的是 a scientific approach，意指\"科学方法/科学化思路\"，而非 high\ntechnology；19 世纪的家政科学化不等于现代高科技应用，前者强调方法、后者强调\n器物。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 housekeeping became a matter of interest to the educated classes 的主语是\nhousekeeping（家政），主语被偷换为 kitchen；并且\"受教育阶层产生兴趣\"是社会背\n景而非贝彻姐妹的主张本身。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只说贝彻姐妹\"倡导科学方法\"，并未把厨房定位成 to experiment with new\nideas；科学方法在文中是被推荐的现成方案，而不是被实验的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What do we learn about today's kitchen?", "options": ["A) It represents the rapid technological advance in people's daily life.", "B) Many of its central features are no different from those of the 1920s.", "C) It has been transformed beyond recognition.", "D) Many of its functions have changed greatly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about today\\'s kitchen? 问的是关于当下厨房可从文中得到的具体信\n息，仍走定位—比对路径。\n【拆句】题干是 What do we learn about today\\'s kitchen?——主句一目了然，关键在末段那句\n对今昔的对比，关系到 many elements remain 这种含\"保持/延续\"语义的动词。\n【定位】It was a modernist triumph, and many elements remain central features of today\\'s kitchen.\n那是一次现代主义的胜利，其许多元素至今仍是当今厨房的核心特征。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说\"1920 年代的许多元素仍是今天厨房的核心\"，重在\"延续\"，未涉及 the rapid\ntechnological advance；恰恰相反，强调的是没有大变。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmany elements remain central features of today\\'s kitchen ↔\nMany of its central features are no different from those of the 1920s.\ndesigned in the 1920s by Margarete Schütte-Lihotsky ↔ those of the 1920s\n预判得到的\"延续语义\"与 no different from those of the 1920s 的\"与那时无差异\"是等\n价表述，可直接选 B。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt has been transformed beyond recognition. 表达\"已被改变得认不出来\"，与原文\nmany elements remain 的\"仍然是\"语义直接对立。\nD) 与原文相反\nMany of its functions have changed greatly. 强调\"功能发生重大改变\"，原文聚焦的\n是 central features\"核心特征延续\"，前者\"功能巨变\"与后者\"特征不变\"在方向上相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2016-06-1"});
